CaCO3 (s) + 2HCl (aq) → CaCl2 (aq) + CO2 (g) + H2O (l)
The reaction is an acid-base reaction because the calcium carbonate is a strong base (it can donate protons) and the hydrochloric acid is a strong acid (it can accept protons).
The reaction can be observed by the formation of a white precipitate of calcium chloride (CaCl2) and the evolution of carbon dioxide gas. The reaction can also be observed by the formation of a white precipitate of calcium chloride (CaCl2) and the evolution of carbon dioxide gas
